{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 12,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.086,
            0.847,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "？ Can we say that any number made up of only the digits ‘0’ and ‘9’, in any order, will always be divisible by 9?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.129,
            0.848,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "Yes, if each digit is either 0 or 9, then each term in its expanded form will be $9 \\times  口   or  \\tilde{0} \\times  口 ( the   口$ denotes a place value). This means each term will be a multiple of 9, for example,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.241,
            0.191,
            0.753,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": "99009=9\\times10000+9\\times1000+0\\times100+0\\times10+9\\times1.",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.215,
            0.709,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "But this shortcut alone cannot identify all the multiples of 9. Unlike the numbers 2, 5, and 10, we cannot identify the multiples of 9 by just looking at the unit’s digit. 99 and 109 are two numbers with 9 as the units digit; but 99 is divisible by 9, while 109 is not."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.728,
            0.211,
            0.846,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.31,
            0.639,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": "Is 10 divisible by 9? If not, what is the remainder?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.334,
            0.774,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "Check the divisibility of other multiples of 10 (10, 20, 30, ...) by 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.354,
            0.846,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "You will notice that for any multiple of 10, the remainder is the same as the number of tens."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.394,
            0.846,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "? Similarly, look at the remainder when the multiples of 100 (100, 200, 300, … ) are divided by 9. What do you notice?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.437,
            0.846,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "The remainder is the same as the number of hundreds for any multiple of 100."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.477,
            0.814,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "？ Using this observation, find the remainder when 427 is divided by 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.505,
            0.61,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.65,
            0.848,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "We see that 427 has 4 hundreds; thus, its corresponding remainder (upon division by 9) would be 4. 427 has 2 tens, and its corresponding remainder would be 2. We have 7 units also remaining. Adding all the remainders, we get 4 + 2 + 7 = 13. We can make one more group of 9 with 13, leaving a remainder of 4. Therefore, 427 ÷ 9 gives a remainder of 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.345,
            0.753,
            0.664,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.451,
                0.899,
                0.545,
                0.915
              ],
              "content": "(Remainder)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
